[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug, one fix. Read the reports, not the subject lines, and
#4471 holds three problems. Each needs its own fix, so each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not known, and not one issue. The queued parser fix does not cover the booking-v3 reports or
the one-day moves.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

The counts come from `reports-4471.json` at render time. The export repeats some reports (its
pages overlap), so a report counts once by its id.

## The three problems

**Month read first.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the
day first, so any date with a day of 12 or less comes out flipped: 5 June becomes 6 May. These
reports name both dates, and the day and month are swapped every time. A few name no date at
all ("wrong day", "a setting for my country writes the day first"); they come from the same
tenants and channels, so they count here.

**MM/DD hint on booking-v3.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.
Its three tenants write the day first, so their people type the month first, as the hint asks,
and the form reads it the tenant's way. The reports read exactly like the parser's, but the fix
is in the form, so the parser fix does not close them.

**Moved by one day.** These dates are not flipped; they move by a day: Tuesday shows as Monday,
one city gets a different day than another, an invite is stamped before its booking was made.
That points at time zones, not at date order. The desk marked them when it read them
(`desk-notes.json`), then filed them by tenant, under the parser. The table counts them the
desk's way, under the parser, and again on their own row, so they have a ticket of their own.

## Why the subject lines hid this

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. All three
problems arrive under the same dozen subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Month and day
swapped?"), so all three landed in #4471. The cause view below shows the split the kb already
computed, and the one-day reports still filed under `parser`.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- Read the body before you merge a large ticket. The subject tells you that a date is wrong,
  not how.
- Intake's tag follows the reporter's browser (`en-US` → `hint`). The tenant decides the
  cause; trust the tables, not the tag.

